== History ==

=== Formation ===

The Badweyn Rural Administration was constituted in 1967 under the [[Administration Reorganization Act (Burawa)|Administration Reorganization Act]], which reorganised Burawa's administrative divisions into the current three-tier system. The administration was carved from the historic [[Sago]] region, with its boundaries drawn to encompass the coastal agricultural lands.

The name "Badweyn" derives from the Burawi words for "Big Ocean," reflecting the administration's extensive coastline on the Aethiopean Ocean.

=== Political developments ===

Unlike neighbouring Dafar Sare and Dafar Hoose, Badweyn has maintained a more diverse political landscape. The [[National Party of Burawa|National Party]] has historically been the dominant force in the administration, consistent with the broader Dafar region's conservative character. However, the [[Christian Socialist Union]] and [[Green Party of Burawa|Green Party]] have both secured representation in the [[Lower House of Burawa|Lower House]].

== Government and politics ==

As a rural administration, Badweyn is governed by an administrator who serves a six-year term. The administrator is nominated by the senator and confirmed by a vote of the communes within the administration. The incumbent administrator is [[Warsame Guuleed]] of the [[National Party of Burawa|National Party]], appointed in 2020.

=== Senate representation ===

{{main|Senate of Burawa}}

Badweyn holds one seat in the [[Senate of Burawa]]. The incumbent senator is [[Siciid Dhegey]] of the [[National Party of Burawa|National Party]], first elected in 2015.

